cyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Petronilla of Aragon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Petronilla was queen of Aragon from 1137 to 1164, the daughter and heiress of Ramiro II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-countess-of-bigorre"><span class="enc-name">Petronilla, Countess of Bigorre</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Petronilla was countess of Bigorre from 1194 until her death in 1251.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/petrozavodsk"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/petrozavodsk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Petrozavodsk</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Petrozavodsk is a city on the western shore of Lake Onega and the capital of the Republic of Karelia in Russia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/petru-aron"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/petru-aron.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Petru Aron</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Petru Aron was Prince of Moldavia three times between 1451 and 1457, during the civil wars that followed the death of Alexander the Good.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/petru-i-of-moldavia"><span class="enc-name">Petru I of Moldavia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Petru I of Moldavia, known as Petru Mușat, was Prince of Moldavia from about 1375 until about 1391 and the first prince of the Mușatin dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/petru-rares"><span class="enc-name">Petru Rareș</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Petru Rareș was Prince of Moldavia from 1527 to 1538 and again from 1541 to 1546, an illegitimate son of Stephen the Great.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/petsamo"><span class="enc-name">Petsamo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Petsamo is a region on the Arctic coast near the Norwegian border, now the Pechengsky district of Russia's Murmansk Oblast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/petuaria"><span class="enc-name">Petuaria</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Petuaria was a Roman town at Brough on the Humber in the East Riding of Yorkshire, England, listed by Ptolemy as the town of the Parisi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/petun"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/petun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Petun</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Petun, or Tionontati, were an Iroquoian people living south of Georgian Bay in what is now Ontario, Canada, neighbours and close allies of the Wendat.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/peuce"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/peuce.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Peuce</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Peuce was an island in the Danube delta known to Greek and Roman writers, named after its pine trees and lying between the river's arms in modern Romania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/peucetius"><span class="enc-name">Peucetius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Peucetius is a figure of Greek legend, a son of the Arcadian king Lycaon, said to have led Arcadian settlers to Italy with his brother Oenotrus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/peugeot"><span class="enc-name">Peugeot</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Peugeot is a French car maker founded by an industrial family from the Montbéliard area, Lutherans like most of the region's people.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pevensey-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pevensey-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pevensey Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pevensey Castle is a medieval castle in East Sussex, England, built inside the walls of the Roman fort of Anderitum.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pfaffenbrief"><span class="enc-name">Pfaffenbrief</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pfaffenbrief, or Priests' Charter, was an agreement of 1370 between Zürich, Lucerne, Zug, Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pfalzgrafenstein-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pfalzgrafenstein-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pfalzgrafenstein Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Pfalzgrafenstein Castle is a toll castle on a small island in the Rhine near Kaub, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pforzheim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pforzheim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pforzheim</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pforzheim is a city at the northern edge of the Black Forest in Baden-Württemberg, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pha-mueang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pha-mueang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pha Mueang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pha Mueang, or Pho Khun P